<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Massa and Carrara</h1><p class="st-meta">1473 CE – 1829 CE · capital Massa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The marble-quarrying state of the Malaspina and Cybo families on the Tuscan coast, whose white stone supplied Michelangelo and Europe's sculptors.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaspina">Malaspina</a>, a feudal family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lunigiana">Lunigiana</a>, ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massa-tuscany">Massa</a>, and in 1473 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacopo-malaspina">Jacopo Malaspina</a> bought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carrara">Carrara</a> with its marble quarries. Through the marriage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ricciarda-malaspina">Ricciarda Malaspina</a>, the state passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-cybo-malaspina">Cybo</a> family, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alberico-i-cybo-malaspina">Alberico I Cybo-Malaspina</a> was made prince in 1568; it became a duchy in 1664.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The rulers governed a narrow strip between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apuan-alps">Apuan Alps</a> and the sea. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carrara">Carrara's</a> marble, quarried since Roman times, was the state's wealth; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michelangelo">Michelangelo</a> chose blocks there. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alberico-i-cybo-malaspina">Alberico I</a> laid out a new town centre at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massa-tuscany">Massa</a>. The state survived as an imperial fief protected by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and its neighbours.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The heiress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-beatrice-d-este-duchess-of-massa">Maria Beatrice Cybo d'Este</a> married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archduke-ferdinand-karl-of-austria">Ferdinand of Austria-Este</a>. After Napoleonic rule under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elisa-bonaparte">Elisa Bonaparte</a>, she was restored in 1814, and on her death in 1829 the duchy passed to her son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-iv-duke-of-modena">Francis IV</a>, joining the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-modena-and-reggio">Duchy of Modena and Reggio</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-massa-and-carrara.jpg" alt="Duchy of Massa and Carrara" width="320" height="197"><figcaption>Image: Germenfer · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Massa_and_Carrara.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1473 CE – 1829 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Massa</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Jacopo Malaspina, Alberico I Cybo-Malaspina, Maria Beatrice Cybo d'Este</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Italian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Principality of Massa, Massa and Carrara</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1651&amp;focus=duchy-of-massa-and-carrara" aria-label="Duchy of Massa and Carrara on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4923 618 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Massa and Carrara lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5243 874L5229 694M5243 874L5261 855"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5261 855h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5243 874h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5243 874h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Massa. <a href="/atlas?year=1651&amp;focus=duchy-of-massa-and-carrara">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
